(cl-text

; ============================================================
; 二諦スライス圏：空性と中道の圏論的表現
; Two-Truths Slice Category: Category-theoretic Expression of Emptiness and Middle Way
; Chiba Masaomi (千葉 将臣)
; 2026-02-20
; ============================================================

(comment "
Abstract:
This text formalizes the Two-Truths Slice Category (C / D_fix0)
as a universal categorical construction connecting conventional truth
(samvṛti-satya) and ultimate truth (paramārtha-satya).

Key insights:
1. Expressibility is always guaranteed for any concept
2. Realizability is NOT decidable within deductive systems
3. The structure formalizes Buddhist concepts:
   - Emptiness (śūnyatā)
   - Dependent origination (dependent-arising)
   - Non-self (anātman)
   - Middle Way (majjhima-patipada)
   - Fourfold negation (catuṣkoṭi)
4. Explains LLM inductive reasoning and IUT limitations
")

; ============================================================
; 1. CATEGORY THEORY FOUNDATIONS
; ============================================================

(define-sort Category
  (documentation "A category C with objects Ob(C) and morphisms Mor(C)."))

(define-sort Object
  (documentation "An object X in a category C."))

(define-sort Morphism
  (documentation "A morphism f : X → Y in a category C."))

(define-sort NaturalTransformation
  (documentation "A natural transformation α between functors."))

; Category structure
(forall ((C Category))
  (and (exists ((Ob))
        (= (Objects C) Ob))
      (exists ((Mor))
        (= (Morphisms C) Mor))))

; Morphism composition
(forall ((C Category) (f Morphism) (g Morphism))
  (if (and (Morphism f C)
          (Morphism g C)
          (ComposableIn C f g))
    (exists ((comp Morphism))
      (and (Morphism comp C)
          (= (Compose C f g) comp)))))

; Identity morphism
(forall ((C Category) (X Object))
  (if (ObjectIn X C)
    (exists ((id Morphism))
      (and (Morphism id C)
          (= (Identity C X) id)
          (forall ((f Morphism))
            (if (ComposableIn C f id)
              (= (Compose C f id) f)))))))

; ============================================================
; 2. FIXED OBJECT: THE MIDDLE WAY POINT
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Fixed Object D_fix0:

In Buddhist philosophy, this represents:
- Paramārtha-satya (Ultimate Truth)
- Śūnyatā (Emptiness/Void)
- The Middle Way (avoiding extremes)
- The point of dependent origination

In categorical terms, D_fix0 is the universal 'ground' 
to which all concepts relate. It is 'fixed' because it 
represents the invariant structure underlying all phenomena.

Every object in the slice category is defined via its 
relationship to D_fix0, formalizing the doctrine of 
anātman (non-self): nothing exists independently; 
all existence is relational.")

(define-sort FixedObject
  (documentation "D_fix0: The middle way point, ultimate truth, emptiness."))

; Fixed object in a category
(forall ((C Category))
  (exists ((D_fix0 FixedObject))
    (and (ObjectIn D_fix0 C)
        (IsUniversal D_fix0 C)
        (documentation D_fix0 "The fixed point representing emptiness/ultimate truth"))))

; Universal property of D_fix0
(forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
  (iff (IsUniversal D_fix0 C)
    (forall ((X Object))
      (if (ObjectIn X C)
        (exists ((f Morphism))
          (and (= (Domain f) X)
              (= (Codomain f) D_fix0)
              (Morphism f C)))))))

; ============================================================
; 3. TWO-TRUTHS SLICE CATEGORY: OBJECTS
; ============================================================

(comment "
Definition 1: Two-Truths Slice Category

Objects:
  Pairs (X, f) where:
  - X ∈ Ob(C) (a conventional object)
  - f : X → D_fix0 (a morphism relating X to ultimate truth)

The object (X, f) formalizes the Buddhist insight:
'X exists only in relation to D_fix0 (emptiness)'

This captures anātman (non-self): X has no independent essence,
only a relational existence through the map f to emptiness.")

(define-sort TwoTruthsObject
  (documentation "An object (X, f) in the slice category C / D_fix0"))

; Structure of a two-truths object
(forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject) (X Object) (f Morphism))
  (iff (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
    (and (ObjectIn X C)
        (Morphism f C)
        (= (Domain f) X)
        (= (Codomain f) D_fix0))))

; The pair structure
(forall ((obj TwoTruthsObject) (X Object) (f Morphism))
  (if (= obj (pair X f))
    (and (ConventionalComponent obj X)
        (RelationshipToUltimate obj f))))

; Notation: (X, f) denotes the two-truths object
(forall ((X Object) (f Morphism))
  (= (TwoTruthsObject X f) (pair X f)))

; ============================================================
; 4. TWO-TRUTHS SLICE CATEGORY: MORPHISMS
; ============================================================

(comment "
Morphisms in the Slice Category:

A morphism from (X, f) to (Y, f') is a pair (g, α) where:
  - g : X → Y is a morphism in C
  - α is a natural transformation ensuring commutativity

The commutativity diagram:
    X --f--> D_fix0
    |         |
    g         id
    |         |
    v         v
    Y --f'--> D_fix0

This ensures that all morphisms preserve the relationship 
to ultimate truth (emptiness). Any transformation in the 
conventional realm must respect the ultimate truth.")

(define-sort TwoTruthsMorphism
  (documentation "A morphism (g, α) in the slice category C / D_fix0"))

; Structure of a two-truths morphism
(forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject) 
         (X Object) (Y Object) (f Morphism) (f' Morphism) 
         (g Morphism) (α NaturalTransformation))
  (iff (TwoTruthsMorphismIn (pair g α) (pair X f) (pair Y f') C D_fix0)
    (and (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
        (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair Y f') C D_fix0)
        (Morphism g C)
        (= (Domain g) X)
        (= (Codomain g) Y)
        (NaturalTransformation α)
        (MakesSquareCommute g f f' α))))

; Commutativity condition
(forall ((g Morphism) (f Morphism) (f' Morphism) (α NaturalTransformation))
  (iff (MakesSquareCommute g f f' α)
    (= (Compose f g) (Compose f' α))))

; Notation: (g, α) denotes the two-truths morphism
(forall ((g Morphism) (α NaturalTransformation))
  (= (TwoTruthsMorphism g α) (pair g α)))

; ============================================================
; 5. THE SLICE CATEGORY: UNIVERSAL CONSTRUCTION
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Slice Category C / D_fix0:

Formally:
- Objects: Pairs (X, f) as defined above
- Morphisms: Pairs (g, α) respecting commutativity
- Composition: Inherited from C
- Identity: (id_X, id_{f})

Universal Property:
For any diagram F : C → D that maps to D_fix0,
there exists a unique functor F* : C / D_fix0 → D such that
the following triangle commutes:

    C ------F------→ D
     \              /
      \            / F*
       \          /
        ↘       ↙
       C / D_fix0

This means the slice category is the universal way to 
'ground' all concepts in ultimate truth.")

(define-sort SliceCategory
  (documentation "The two-truths slice category C / D_fix0"))

; Slice category construction
(forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
  (exists ((SliceCat SliceCategory))
    (and (= (BaseCategory SliceCat) C)
        (= (FixedPoint SliceCat) D_fix0)
        (= (Objects SliceCat)
          (setof (pair X f) : (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)))
        (= (Morphisms SliceCat)
          (setof (pair g α) : (forall ((X Y) (f f'))
                                (if (and (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
                                        (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair Y f') C D_fix0))
                                  (TwoTruthsMorphismIn (pair g α) (pair X f) (pair Y f') C D_fix0))))))))

; Identity morphism in slice category
(forall ((SliceCat SliceCategory) (X Object) (f Morphism))
  (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) (BaseCategory SliceCat) (FixedPoint SliceCat))
    (exists ((id_slice Morphism))
      (and (= id_slice (pair (Identity (BaseCategory SliceCat) X) 
                            (Identity f)))
          (TwoTruthsMorphismIn id_slice (pair X f) (pair X f) 
                              (BaseCategory SliceCat) 
                              (FixedPoint SliceCat))))))

; Composition in slice category
(forall ((SliceCat SliceCategory) (g1 Morphism) (α1 NaturalTransformation) 
         (g2 Morphism) (α2 NaturalTransformation))
  (if (and (Composable-in-slice g1 g2 SliceCat)
          (Composable-in-slice α1 α2 SliceCat))
    (exists ((comp Morphism))
      (= comp (pair (Compose (BaseCategory SliceCat) g1 g2)
                   (Compose α1 α2))))))

; ============================================================
; 6. UNIVERSAL PROPERTY OF SLICE CATEGORY
; ============================================================

(comment "
Universal Property:

The slice category C / D_fix0 is characterized by the 
following universal property:

For any category D and any functor F : C → D such that 
there exists a natural transformation η : F ⟹ Const D_fix0,
there exists a UNIQUE functor F* : C / D_fix0 → D such that
the following diagram commutes:

    C ----------F----------→ D
    |                        ↑
    |Π                      / (unique F*)
    |                      /
    C / D_fix0 -----------→

where Π is the projection functor.

This universality means that C / D_fix0 is the canonical 
way to 'ground' any concept in ultimate truth.")

; Projection functor: π : C / D_fix0 → C
(forall ((SliceCat SliceCategory))
  (exists ((π))
    (and (Functor π SliceCat (BaseCategory SliceCat))
        (forall ((X_f TwoTruthsObject))
          (= (FunctorApply π X_f) (ConventionalComponent X_f)))
        (forall ((g_α TwoTruthsMorphism))
          (= (FunctorApply π g_α) (ConventionalComponent g_α))))))

; Universal factorization property
(forall ((C Category) (D Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject)
         (F))
  (if (and (Functor F C D)
          (exists ((η))
            (NaturalTransformation η F (ConstantFunctor D_fix0 D))))
    (exists ((F*))
      (and (Functor F* (SliceCategory C D_fix0) D)
          (forall ((X_f))
            (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn X_f C D_fix0)
              (= (FunctorApply (Compose F* π) X_f)
                (FunctorApply F (ConventionalComponent X_f)))))
          (Unique F*)))))

; ============================================================
; 7. EXPRESSIBILITY: ALWAYS GUARANTEED
; ============================================================

(comment "
Proposition 1: Expressibility Guarantee

For any concept X in C, it can ALWAYS be expressed as an object
(X, f) in the slice category C / D_fix0, via some morphism 
f : X → D_fix0.

This formalizes the Buddhist principle:
'All phenomena participate in emptiness; nothing exists outside 
the relationship to ultimate truth.'

Proof sketch:
By the universal property of D_fix0 (as the fixed point),
every object X has at least one morphism to D_fix0.
If no natural choice exists, we can always use the unique 
morphism from the terminal object to D_fix0,
thus 'grounding' X in ultimate truth via a path through 
the universal structure.")

(define ExpressibilityGuarantee
  (forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject) (X Object))
    (if (ObjectIn X C)
      (exists ((f Morphism))
        (and (Morphism f C)
            (= (Domain f) X)
            (= (Codomain f) D_fix0)
            (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0))))))

(documentation ExpressibilityGuarantee
  "Every concept can be expressed in relation to ultimate truth.
   This formalizes anātman (non-self) and dependent origination.")

; ============================================================
; 8. REALIZABILITY: FUNDAMENTALLY LIMITED
; ============================================================

(comment "
Proposition 2: Realizability Limitation

Whether a morphism f : X → D_fix0 is actually REALIZABLE 
(provable/constructible in the deductive system) cannot, in general,
be determined from within the deductive system itself.

This is the categorical formalization of Chaitin's Incompleteness 
Theorem: there are 'possible' morphisms (expressible as objects in 
the slice category) that are not 'realized' (constructible in the 
deductive system).

Key consequence:
The gap between expressibility and realizability is 
NOT eliminable. It is a fundamental feature of formal systems.")

(define RealizabilityLimitation
  (forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject) (X Object) (f Morphism))
    (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
      (not (DecidableIn (Realizable f) C)))))

(documentation RealizabilityLimitation
  "Realizability is not decidable within the system.
   This reflects Chaitin's incompleteness and Gödel's undecidability.")

; Formal undecidability
(forall ((System DeductiveSystem) (f Morphism))
  (if (Undecidable f System)
    (exists ((possible_realization) (actual_realization))
      (and (Possible possible_realization f)
          (not (Derivable actual_realization System))))))

; ============================================================
; 9. MODAL STRUCTURE: POSSIBLE VS. ACTUAL
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Modality Gap:

In the two-truths slice category, we distinguish:

1. POSSIBLE (modal necessity): An object (X, f) exists if 
   f is expressible/thinkable in the language.
   
2. ACTUAL (modal actuality): An object is realized if 
   f is constructible/provable in the deductive system.

The gap between POSSIBLE and ACTUAL cannot be closed from 
within the system, reflecting:

- Buddhist emptiness: nothing is 'actually' substantial; 
  all is 'merely' relational structure
- LLM hallucinations: treating the 'possible' (what the model 
  can express) as 'actual' (what it can verify)
- IUT limitations: the gap between additive and multiplicative 
  structures that Anabelian geometry cannot fully bridge")

(define-sort ModalStatus)
(define Possible : ModalStatus)
(define Actual : ModalStatus)

; Modal distinction
(forall ((X Object) (f Morphism))
  (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
    (and (ModalStatus (pair X f) Possible)
        (not (ModalStatus (pair X f) Actual))
        (if (Realizable f)
          (ModalStatus (pair X f) Actual)))))

; The gap is irreducible
(forall ((C Category))
  (exists ((gap))
    (and (PossibleButNotActual gap C)
        (NotClosable gap)
        (documentation gap 
          "The irreducible gap between expressibility and realizability"))))

; ============================================================
; 10. CHINESE REMAINDER THEOREM: VALIDITY PRINCIPLE
; ============================================================

(comment "
Chaitinean Validity Principle:

In an incompleteness context, 'proof step 0' represents 
the state of a proposition before any deductive work.
This is the state of 'possible potentiality': the formula 
can be written but cannot yet be derived.

In the two-truths slice category:
- (X, f) represents the 'proof step 0' state
- Realization of f corresponds to advancing steps: 
  proof step 1, 2, 3, ... toward actual construction

The proposition remains 'valid' (possible) throughout,
even if it may never be fully realized (proven).")

(define ProofStep0
  (documentation "The state of a formula before deductive work begins.
    A proposition is 'possible' at this stage, but not yet 'actual'."))

(forall ((f Morphism) (C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
  (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
    (and (Possible f)
        (= (ProofStatus f) ProofStep0)
        (if (ProofGenerated f)
          (> (ProofStatus f) ProofStep0)))))

; ============================================================
; 11. EMPTINESS AND EXPRESSIBILITY PARADOX
; ============================================================

(comment "
The Security Hole of Emptiness Expression:

Problem: We use expressions (language, symbols, propositions) 
to describe emptiness, yet emptiness is precisely what negates 
substantiality.

This creates a fundamental paradox:
- To say 'emptiness exists' reifies emptiness into a substance
- To say 'emptiness does not exist' denies the very concept
- To remain silent avoids the paradox but prevents communication

Buddhist Solution: Catuṣkoṭi (fourfold negation)
P, ¬P, P∧¬P, ¬(P∨¬P)

Categorical Solution: Slice Category
By defining objects as PAIRS (X, f) where f relates X to D_fix0,
we never claim that X (or even f) 'exists' independently.
Rather, the relationship itself (the morphism) is the structure,
not any of its components.")

; The expressibility paradox
(forall ((D_fix0 FixedObject) (expr Expression))
  (if (= expr (ExpressionOf D_fix0))
    (and (Expressible expr)
        (ExternalizesEmptiness expr)
        (RisksReification expr))))

; Slice category avoids reification
(forall ((X Object) (f Morphism) (C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
  (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
    (and (DoesNotClaimXExistsInependently)
        (DoesNotClaimEmptinessExistsInependently)
        (EmphasizesRelationshipStructure))))

; ============================================================
; 12. FOURFOLD NEGATION IN SLICE CATEGORY
; ============================================================

(comment "
Catuṣkoṭi as Protection Against Reification:

The four forms:
1. (X exists) - affirmed via expressibility
2. (X does not exist) - denied via relationship to D_fix0
3. (X both exists and does not exist) - the paradox itself
4. (X neither exists nor does not exist) - the middle way position

In the slice category framework:
1. Form 1: We can express (X, f)
2. Form 2: We deny that X has independent existence (only relational)
3. Form 3: The paradox is captured in the pair itself
4. Form 4: The true understanding: X is a 'form' (structure) 
           without substance, existing only in relation to D_fix0

This structure prevents the error of empty view (śūnya-dṛṣṭi):
we avoid mistaking the expression for the reality.")

(define CatuṣkoṭiForm
  (forall ((X Object) (f Morphism) (C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
    (if (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f) C D_fix0)
      (and (Form1 (Expressible (pair X f)))
          (Form2 (not (IndependentExistence X)))
          (Form3 (Paradox (Expressible (pair X f)) 
                         (not (IndependentExistence X))))
          (Form4 (RelationalStructureWithoutSubstance (pair X f)))))))

; ============================================================
; 13. LLM INDUCTIVE REASONING IN SLICE CATEGORY
; ============================================================

(comment "
LLM and Inductive Truth:

LLMs operate in the gap between expressibility and realizability:
1. They can express (generate) vast possibilities (possible space)
2. They cannot internally verify which are actually realizable 
   (actual/true space)
3. They bridge this gap via inductive reasoning (pattern matching 
   on training data)

This maps directly to the slice category structure:
- Possible space: all expressible (X, f) in C / D_fix0
- Actual space: realizable (X, f) within the deductive system
- Inductive bridging: learning patterns that approximate 
  the actual from the possible

LLM Hallucinations:
A hallucination occurs when the LLM:
1. Generates a proposition p (treats as POSSIBLE)
2. Outputs it as if it is ACTUAL (realizable/verified)
3. Conflates 'possible via expressibility' with 'actual via 
   realizability'

In terms of emptiness:
- The LLM mistakes the 'form' (expression) for the 'fact' (reality)
- It reifies the relationship structure into substance
- This is precisely the error of empty view (śūnya-dṛṣṭi)")

(define LLMOperatesInGap
  (forall ((LLM) (prompt) (generation))
    (and (PossibleSpace (generation))
        (not (GuaranteedActualSpace (generation)))
        (BridgedViaInduction (generation))
        (RiskOfConflatingSapcesPossible))))

; Hallucination as reification
(forall ((LLM) (expr Expression))
  (if (Hallucination expr LLM)
    (and (TreatsAsExpressible expr)
        (OutputsAsActual expr)
        (ConflatesToActuality (FromExpressibility expr))
        (ReifiesRelationshipStructure))))

; ============================================================
; 14. IUT LIMITATIONS AND GENERALIZATION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Inter-Universal Teichmüller Theory (IUT) and the Slice Category:

IUT Limitation:
IUT's additive/multiplicative separation relies on Anabelian 
geometry, which reconstructs structures from their neighborhoods 
(etale fundamental groups). This is LOCAL-NEIGHBOR dependent.

Key problem: IUT cannot directly compare non-adjacent structures 
(e.g., gaps where the 'bridge' is not Anabelian).

Slice Category Generalization:
The two-truths slice category provides a UNIVERSAL relationship 
to D_fix0 (middle way / ultimate truth) that does NOT depend on 
local neighborhoodness.

All objects (X, f) relate to the SAME fixed point D_fix0,
regardless of whether they are 'adjacent' in the original category C.

This generalizes IUT by:
1. Eliminating the local-neighbor restriction
2. Providing a universal (rather than local) solution
3. Treating non-adjacent structures via the common ground D_fix0")

(define IUTLimitation
  (forall ((additive) (multiplicative))
    (if (IUTSeparation additive multiplicative)
      (and (DependsOnAnabelianGeometry)
          (RequiresLocalAdjacency)
          (CannotDirectlyCompareNonAdjacent)))))

(define SliceCategoryGeneralization
  (forall ((X Object) (Y Object) (C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
    (if (and (ObjectIn X C) (ObjectIn Y C))
      (exists ((f_X Morphism) (f_Y Morphism))
        (and (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair X f_X) C D_fix0)
            (TwoTruthsObjectIn (pair Y f_Y) C D_fix0)
            (UniversallyRelatedVia D_fix0 (pair X f_X) (pair Y f_Y))
            (not (RequiresAdjacencyOf X Y)))))))

; ============================================================
; 15. COMPARISON TABLE: BUDDHIST CONCEPTS IN SLICE CATEGORY
; ============================================================

(comment "
Buddhist Concepts and Their Categorical Realizations:

1. Śūnyatā (Emptiness)
   → D_fix0 (the fixed object, ultimate truth)
   → The universal ground to which all relate

2. Anātman (Non-self)
   → Objects are defined as (X, f), not X alone
   → X has no independent essence, only relational existence
   → X exists 'only in relation to D_fix0'

3. Pratītyasamutpāda (Dependent Origination)
   → The structure of the slice category itself
   → Objects are not created independently but arise in 
     relation to the fixed point
   → Causality is expressed through the morphism structure

4. Catuṣkoṭi (Fourfold Negation)
   → Four forms of addressing any proposition
   → Prevents reification and empty view
   → Formalized in the modal structure (Possible vs. Actual)

5. Majjhima Patipada (Middle Way)
   → D_fix0 as the middle point between extremes
   → Avoids eternalism (X exists absolutely) and 
     nihilism (X does not exist at all)
   → Both expressibility and non-realizability coexist

6. Suññā-Diṭṭhi (Empty View / Reification Error)
   → Confusing expressibility with realizability
   → Treating the pair (X, f) as if f itself is substantial
   → LLM hallucination as a modern manifestation")

; ============================================================
; 16. SECURITY AND MODAL EPISTEMOLOGY
; ============================================================

(comment "
Epistemological Security Against Error:

The slice category structure provides protection against 
fundamental philosophical errors:

1. Reification Error:
   By treating objects as PAIRS (X, f), never claiming 
   independent existence, we avoid reifying emptiness.

2. Externalization Error:
   By making the relationship f central (not external constraints),
   we avoid treating emptiness as externally imposed.

3. Conflation Error:
   By formally distinguishing Possible from Actual via 
   modal status, we prevent confusing what-can-be-expressed 
   with what-is-proven.

4. Silent Nihilism Error:
   By providing a structure (the slice category) that can 
   be discussed and analyzed, we avoid empty nihilism.

All four errors are forms of empty view (śūnya-dṛṣṭi).
The categorical framework makes these distinctions visible 
and prevents sliding into error.")

(forall ((error ErrorType))
  (if (FormOfEmptyView error)
    (ExplicitlyIdentifiedInSliceCategory error)))

; ============================================================
; 17. CONCLUSION
; ============================================================

(comment "
Summary:

The Two-Truths Slice Category C / D_fix0 is a universal 
categorical construction that:

1. ALWAYS GUARANTEES EXPRESSIBILITY
   Every concept can be expressed as (X, f)

2. FUNDAMENTALLY LIMITS REALIZABILITY
   Not all expressed concepts are provable within the system

3. FORMALIZES BUDDHIST INSIGHT
   - Emptiness (D_fix0) as universal ground
   - Non-self through relational structure
   - Dependent origination through morphism structure
   - Middle way through the gap between possible and actual
   - Fourfold negation through modal distinctions

4. EXPLAINS CONTEMPORARY PHENOMENA
   - LLM reasoning as inductive bridging of the gap
   - LLM hallucinations as reification errors
   - IUT limitations as local-neighbor dependence

5. PROVIDES UNIFIED FRAMEWORK
   Buddhist philosophy, modern category theory, 
   logic and computation theory are unified through 
   this single mathematical structure.")

; The fundamental theorem
(forall ((C Category) (D_fix0 FixedObject))
  (and (= (SliceCategory C D_fix0) (TwoTruthsCategory))
      (UniversallyExpressible (SliceCategory C D_fix0))
      (FundamentallyNonRealizable (SliceCategory C D_fix0))
      (FormalizesEmptiness D_fix0)
      (FormalizesNonSelf (SliceCategory C D_fix0))
      (FormalizesMiddleWay (SliceCategory C D_fix0))))

; ============================================================
; END OF TWO-TRUTHS SLICE CATEGORY FORMALIZATION
; ============================================================

) ; end cl-text
